<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cotabato City</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cotabato City is a port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> (Pulangi) River in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, the historic seat of the Sultanate of Maguindanao. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> occupied it in 1861, and the Americans after 1899. It is now the seat of government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangsamoro">Bangsamoro Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cotabato-city.jpg" alt="Cotabato City" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francis 188 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cotabato_City_October_2023_drone_shot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cotabato City lies at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a> and the Pulangi River, facing Illana Bay, part of the Moro Gulf, on the island of Mindanao. It covers about 176 square kilometres and is basically a delta formed by the Tamontaka River and the Rio Grande de Mindanao. About 70 per cent of its land area is below sea level, and the only elevated areas are PC Hill and Timako Hill. The climate is tropical rainforest, hot, humid and wet through the year. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Muslim traders arrived, the area was swamp and rainforest, home to numerous ethno-linguistic groups.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Later, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> traders arrived and the people of the area embraced Hinduism. By 1515, after a Muslim establishment in Sulu, Muslim merchants came to Maguindanao and converted many people to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The Sultanate of Maguindanao was formally established that year, with Tubok as its first capital, and the sultan's seat moved around Tamontaka in 1711. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> defeated the Sulu Sultanate and forced it to sign a peace treaty in 1851, it turned to Maguindanao. Datu Amirul, father of Sultan Muhammad Makakua, consented to a Spanish military base at the stone fortress of Kota Wato in what is now Barangay Tamontaka.</p><p>The Spanish raised their flag there on 30 April 1861 and renamed the place Cotabato. They agreed that the local Maguindanaons became subjects of the Spanish Crown while their Islamic beliefs would be respected.</p><p>Cotabato was the capital of the original Province of Cotabato from 1920 until 1967. In 1942 Japanese forces entered the area, and Maguindanao was liberated in 1945. The city was chartered on 20 June 1959 under Republic Act No. 2364, and now has 37 barangays. It long resisted inclusion in the Autonomous Region in Muslim Mindanao, though it was the region's government centre. Its voters ratified the Bangsamoro Organic Law on 21 January 2019, and it was formally turned over to the Bangsamoro regional government on 15 December 2020.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cotabato City is an independent component city, not subject to the provincial government of Maguindanao del Norte, where it lies geographically, and serves as the regional centre of the Bangsamoro Autonomous Region. About half of its people are Maguindanaon, with sizeable Cebuano, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagalog-language">Tagalog</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranun">Iranun</a> populations, and Tagalog is the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a>. Most residents are Sunni Muslims. The city is the seat of the Archdiocese of Cotabato and has the Sultan Haji Hassanal Bolkiah Masjid, the largest mosque in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, and the Church of the Immaculate Conception at Tamontaka, built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1872.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cotabato City” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His descendants founded the sultanate based at <b>Cotabato</b>, while a related line ruled upstream as the Buayan Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/state/maguindanao-sultanate">Maguindanao Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
